WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.160
What happens to Wanda by the end of&nbsp;
the film? Who was that character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.320
the credits sequence? And everyone is&nbsp;
asking 'where's Kang?' These are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.920
biggest unanswered questions from "Doctor&nbsp;
Strange in the Multiverse of Madness."

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.400
Right from the top, "Doctor Strange in&nbsp;
the Multiverse of Madness" drops a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.360
of intriguing details about how the multiverse&nbsp;
works. Past MCU installments like "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.520
Endgame" and "Loki" have explored the&nbsp;
idea of variants — alternate versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.680
of characters from different realities —&nbsp;
but this film takes things a step further.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.160
In the film, it's implied that dreams are&nbsp;
windows into other universes, at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.720
insofar as it applies to magical characters&nbsp;
like Stephen Strange and Wanda Maximoff.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.160
The movie then upends this concept with the new&nbsp;
character of America Chavez. America has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.120
power to travel through realities, and most of the&nbsp;
film's plot revolves around the desire to control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.360
or seize, these powers. She also mentions that she&nbsp;
has never located any other versions of herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.640
in other universes. She knows this both because&nbsp;
she's looked — but also because she never dreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.000
Do America's powers have something to&nbsp;
do with there only being one of her?

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.360
One of the biggest twists in "Doctor Strange in&nbsp;
the Multiverse of Madness" is the introduction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:15.760
the Illuminati on Earth-838. The secret group is&nbsp;
hugely important to the Marvel comics universe — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.400
council of powerful figures who guide world&nbsp;
events from the shadows and often make difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.520
decisions in the process. The 838 Illuminati&nbsp;
are a staggering collection of variants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.400
Marvel TV characters, previously&nbsp;
non-MCU favorites, and popular fancasts.

00:01:38.459 --> 00:01:44.539
Chiwetel Ejiofor John Krasinski's Reed Richards,&nbsp;
and Patrick Stewart's Professor Charles Xavier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.539 --> 00:01:49.899
It's an impressive roster that also leaves a lot&nbsp;
of hanging questions about the future of the MCU.

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:54.859
For starters, some of the Illuminati members'&nbsp;
individual origin stories are left unaddressed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:59.739
What happened to Steve Rogers in this universe,&nbsp;
and was Captain Carter's origin the same as in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:05.099
"What if...?" More importantly, the Illuminati&nbsp;
makes three major Marvel character groups official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.099 --> 00:02:11.099
in the MCU: the Inhumans, the X-Men, and the&nbsp;
Fantastic Four. It's unknown how each of them came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:16.539
to be in Universe 838, but it seems likely that&nbsp;
they'll all factor into the franchise's future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.539 --> 00:02:21.659
Perhaps a new, cross-dimensional Illuminati&nbsp;
could form. Or maybe Marvel Studios' "Fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.659 --> 00:02:27.099
Four" and "X-Men" films will take place far&nbsp;
away from Earth-616. Only time will tell.

00:02:28.379 --> 00:02:32.779
Of course, the highlight is Patrick Stewart's&nbsp;
appearance as Professor Charles Xavier, a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.779 --> 00:02:38.299
he's played for decades in the "X-Men" movies&nbsp;
that previously existed outside the MCU. While not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:43.259
explicit, Stewart's return via "Doctor Strange in&nbsp;
the Multiverse of Madness" could seem to confirm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.259 --> 00:02:48.779
that all those old X-Men movies are canon within&nbsp;
the MCU multiverse. Nonetheless, the other X-Men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.779 --> 00:02:54.139
aren't mentioned at all in this "Doctor Strange"&nbsp;
sequel, and neither is the word "mutant." As of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:58.699
now, it's unclear how those aspects of the&nbsp;
franchise will be tied back into the MCU.

00:02:58.699 --> 00:03:03.579
There are a lot of options on the table. Stars&nbsp;
of the later X-Men movies could potentially be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.579 --> 00:03:08.779
brought back as variants. Alternatively, the main&nbsp;
"X-Men" characters could be completely recast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:12.379
down the line, with those changes&nbsp;
being justified by the multiverse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:15.979
Marvel Studios and Disney could even&nbsp;
continue the Fox "X-Men" series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.979 --> 00:03:20.539
although that seems like an unlikely option.&nbsp;
Whatever happens, it should be exciting to watch.

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:25.819
One of the pieces of lore introduced in "Doctor&nbsp;
Strange in the Multiverse of Madness" involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:30.379
incursions — cataclysmic events where&nbsp;
multiple realities are smashed together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:36.139
Earth-616's Stephen Strange is informed by the&nbsp;
Illuminati that his 838 counterpart caused an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.139 --> 00:03:42.539
incursion by using the Darkhold to dreamwalk — a&nbsp;
crime punished by a brutal execution. Later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.539 --> 00:03:47.739
Doctor Strange 616 meets a version of himself&nbsp;
who has become deeply corrupted by the Darkhold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:52.619
and is implied to have caused his own incursions.&nbsp;
Finally, at the end of the film, the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.619 --> 00:03:58.779
character Clea shows up and tasks Strange 616&nbsp;
with fixing an incursion she claims he caused.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:01.179
"Things just got out of hand."

00:04:01.899 --> 00:04:06.699
None of these events are explained in detail,&nbsp;
but they could be in further MCU installments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.699 --> 00:04:11.019
The end credits of "Multiverse of Madness"&nbsp;
proclaim that Doctor Strange will return, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:16.139
that next entry could be wilder than ever. "Doctor&nbsp;
Strange 2" spends a lot of time talking about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:21.499
uniquely dangerous its eponymous hero can be, and&nbsp;
if that theme is to remain relevant going forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.499 --> 00:04:25.659
it would make sense for the incursions he&nbsp;
has caused to be explored in greater detail.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:31.179
One of the more bizarre moments in "Doctor Strange&nbsp;
in the Multiverse of Madness" occurs at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.179 --> 00:04:36.219
of the film. when the hero snatches a copy of&nbsp;
the Darkhold from his evil counterpart. He then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.219 --> 00:04:41.019
uses the spells within the tome to dreamwalk&nbsp;
into the corpse of one of his other variants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.019 --> 00:04:44.139
who was buried on Earth-616&nbsp;
at the beginning of the film.

00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:48.379
This doesn't sit well with the spirits of the&nbsp;
dead, however, who quickly rise from their realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.379 --> 00:04:53.579
to accost Doctor Strange. Fortunately, Christine&nbsp;
Palmer is there to help keep him grounded.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.579 --> 00:04:57.019
She reminds him that he's a Master of&nbsp;
the Mystic Arts and encourages him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.019 --> 00:05:01.019
use the spirits of the damned himself.&nbsp;
He does just that, sucking up all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.019 --> 00:05:06.299
ghosties into himself and growing a pair of&nbsp;
shadowy spirit wings on his zombified body.

00:05:06.299 --> 00:05:10.299
It makes for a fun climax, but the movie&nbsp;
doesn't do a great job of actually explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.299 --> 00:05:14.379
what happens. Has Doctor Strange always&nbsp;
had the ability to control the spirits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:18.459
of the dead? Is it a power granted only&nbsp;
to his zombie form, or by the Darkhold?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.459 --> 00:05:23.099
Could any sorcerer potentially wield this&nbsp;
ability? Now that all of the Darkholds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:26.859
supposedly destroyed, we may never get&nbsp;
concrete answers to these questions.

00:05:27.819 --> 00:05:31.979
Underlining how powerful the character is,&nbsp;
in the end, Scarlet Witch isn't defeated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.979 --> 00:05:37.899
Doctor Strange, or Wong, or America Chavez. She's&nbsp;
brought down by her own sense of guilt — a fierce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.899 --> 00:05:43.179
realization of the monster she's become. Face to&nbsp;
face with her sons in an alternate reality, Wanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.179 --> 00:05:49.099
sees them look upon her with fear. In a redemptive&nbsp;
act, she brings the entirety of Mount Wundagore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:54.379
collapsing down upon itself with her inside,&nbsp;
demolishing the ancient dark magic written there.

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:57.259
"That's the book of the damned."

00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:02.619
This destructive act of penitence has&nbsp;
a curious side-effect: The erasure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.619 --> 00:06:07.259
every Darkhold from every corner of the&nbsp;
multiverse. How exactly Wanda manages to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.259 --> 00:06:12.379
pull off that feat, however, is unclear. The&nbsp;
Scarlet Witch has the power to alter reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.379 --> 00:06:15.739
so it's possible that the mountain&nbsp;
magnified her powers in some way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.739 --> 00:06:20.379
and allowed her to affect other universes.&nbsp;
Even then, though, things don't quite add up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:24.699
If there are Darkholds in most universes, it&nbsp;
stands to reason that there are other versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.699 --> 00:06:29.259
of Mount Wundagore as well. Couldn't someone&nbsp;
from one of those realities simply transcribe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:33.499
another book? Or did Wanda's magic erase&nbsp;
the mountains in every universe as well?

00:06:34.619 --> 00:06:40.619
Wanda's demolition of the Mount Wundagore temple&nbsp;
would seem like a definitive end to the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.619 --> 00:06:45.499
That is, it would seem that way if Kevin Feige&nbsp;
hadn't already confirmed that she'll be back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.499 --> 00:06:49.659
The nature of her next appearance remains&nbsp;
uncertain, however, and there are a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:54.059
different ways it could play out. The existence of&nbsp;
the multiverse means that the franchise could just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.059 --> 00:06:58.939
lean on a variant of the character, one of whom&nbsp;
plays a major role in "Multiverse of Madness."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.939 --> 00:07:03.099
Given how much time has been spent&nbsp;
building up Earth-616's Wanda, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.099 --> 00:07:07.339
it seems like a missed opportunity not&nbsp;
to continue her story in some capacity.

00:07:07.339 --> 00:07:10.539
At the very least, it seems highly&nbsp;
unlikely that a collapsing mountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.539 --> 00:07:13.979
would be enough to stop the Scarlet&nbsp;
Witch. "Doctor Strange in the Multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:18.539
of Madness" is basically a montage of her&nbsp;
surviving every conceivable sort of attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:22.059
There's no way a few big rocks would&nbsp;
be enough to keep her down ... right?

00:07:22.059 --> 00:07:24.059
"I don't know, probably."

00:07:25.019 --> 00:07:29.019
A lot of the action in "Doctor Strange in the&nbsp;
Multiverse of Madness" involves the Book of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.019 --> 00:07:34.779
Vishanti, an ancient tome of good magic and the&nbsp;
antithesis of the Darkhold. It's believed that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.779 --> 00:07:39.019
book contains spells that could stop the Scarlet&nbsp;
Witch, but unfortunately, that theory is never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.019 --> 00:07:44.219
confirmed. Just as our heroes reach the Book of&nbsp;
Vishanti, Wanda destroys it with her dark magic.

00:07:44.219 --> 00:07:48.859
But is the Book of Vishanti really gone?&nbsp;
The place where it dwells is referred to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.859 --> 00:07:53.899
a world between realities, which suggests&nbsp;
that there is only one copy. Presumably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.899 --> 00:07:56.539
that means Wanda burned the only one in existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.539 --> 00:08:00.939
removing it from the multiverse permanently.&nbsp;
The truth may be a bit more complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:05.259
however. Who's to say that the book didn't&nbsp;
whisk itself off to some other place for safety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.259 --> 00:08:09.659
leaving only the illusion that it was destroyed?&nbsp;
That would be an impressive magic trick.

00:08:10.299 --> 00:08:13.099
"So, this is the magic trick."

00:08:13.099 --> 00:08:13.499
"Illusion, Michael."

00:08:13.499 --> 00:08:18.059
Alternatively, it's possible that another&nbsp;
copy does exist elsewhere. Because the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.059 --> 00:08:21.339
version of the tome never actually gets&nbsp;
used in the "Doctor Strange" sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.339 --> 00:08:25.019
it's tough to theorize too much about&nbsp;
what role it could play going forward.

00:08:25.899 --> 00:08:30.379
At the very end of "Doctor Strange 2,"&nbsp;
Stephen makes his way back to Earth-616&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.379 --> 00:08:35.179
with a new lease on life. But the last shot&nbsp;
before the first round of credits shows a third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:40.699
eye open in his forehead — similar to the one worn&nbsp;
by his sinister counterpart earlier in the film.

00:08:40.699 --> 00:08:45.179
There are a few possible explanations for&nbsp;
Doctor Strange's third eye. The Eye of Agamotto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.179 --> 00:08:49.979
acquired by Strange in his first solo film, has&nbsp;
a number of powers in the comics that haven't yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.979 --> 00:08:55.179
featured in the MCU. The big-screen version of the&nbsp;
Eye is mainly used to hold the Time Stone, but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.179 --> 00:09:00.539
the original Marvel canon, it also brings powers&nbsp;
of transcendent vision and knowledge, among other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:06.299
things. These abilities often manifest themselves&nbsp;
in the form of a third eye. Since Agamotto himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.299 --> 00:09:11.659
is one of the Vishanti in the comics — powerful&nbsp;
godlike beings who aid the Sorcerer Supreme and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.659 --> 00:09:16.379
created the Book of the Vishanti — it's possible&nbsp;
that Strange's interactions with the sacred tome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.379 --> 00:09:21.739
or its opposite, the Darkhold, could have opened&nbsp;
the Eye of Agamotto up to its true potential.

00:09:21.739 --> 00:09:24.219
Alternatively, Doctor Strange&nbsp;
could have simply reached a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.219 --> 00:09:27.579
level of mystical power through the&nbsp;
story of "Multiverse of Madness,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.579 --> 00:09:31.659
thus unlocking the secrets of the Eye.&nbsp;
Either way, he seems stronger than ever.

00:09:32.619 --> 00:09:37.179
The first post-credits scene in "Doctor Strange&nbsp;
in the Multiverse of Madness" introduces Clea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.179 --> 00:09:41.899
a woman from an alternate reality played by&nbsp;
Charlize Theron. She approaches the Stephen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.899 --> 00:09:46.779
Strange of Earth-616 and demands that he help&nbsp;
her fix an incursion he apparently caused.

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:52.139
In the comics, Clea is a denizen of the Dark&nbsp;
Dimension with close ties to Dormammu himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.139 --> 00:09:57.099
Given that her purple hue resembles Dormammu's&nbsp;
MCU incarnation and that the universe she opens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.099 --> 00:10:01.099
a portal to in "Doctor Strange 2" looks&nbsp;
similar to the Dark Dimension in some ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.099 --> 00:10:04.539
it's likely that this backstory will&nbsp;
continue to hold true. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.539 --> 00:10:08.459
she makes no mention of her home or of&nbsp;
Dormammu during the post-credits scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.459 --> 00:10:12.619
so it's yet to be revealed what the nature&nbsp;
of her character will be in the MCU.

00:10:12.619 --> 00:10:16.699
One thing of particular importance to&nbsp;
note is that Clea ends up marrying Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:20.379
Strange in the comics. Since he seems&nbsp;
to have made his peace with the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.379 --> 00:10:24.139
that he'll never settle down happily with&nbsp;
Christine by the end of his second film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.139 --> 00:10:27.659
the MCU could be building a new&nbsp;
love story between him and Clea.

00:10:28.699 --> 00:10:32.619
While not particularly relevant to the&nbsp;
specific story of "Doctor Strange in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.619 --> 00:10:37.579
Multiverse of Madness," Kang the Conqueror&nbsp;
looms large over the whole film. From the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.579 --> 00:10:41.259
dimension-hopping nature of the story&nbsp;
to the introduction of Reed Richards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.259 --> 00:10:46.059
there are tons of plot details that&nbsp;
silently hint at the villain's MCU return.

00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:50.699
At the end of "Loki" Season 1, the God of&nbsp;
Mischief finds himself in a severely altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.699 --> 00:10:55.019
Time Variance Authority that seems to be run&nbsp;
by Kang. Presumably, that means that he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.019 --> 00:10:59.659
be active in his conquest by the events of the&nbsp;
"Doctor Strange" sequel, but it's possible that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:05.019
doesn't yet possess a way to easily travel across&nbsp;
dimensions. In that instance, America Chavez would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.019 --> 00:11:10.059
be of special interest to him, just as she is&nbsp;
to Wanda Maximoff. While it makes sense not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.059 --> 00:11:15.179
overcrowd any one movie with too many characters&nbsp;
or storylines, Kang could nonetheless be impacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.179 --> 00:11:20.059
by the events of "Multiverse of Madness."&nbsp;
He's set to appear in "Ant-Man and the Wasp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.059 --> 00:11:25.499
Quantumania," if not sooner, and it should be&nbsp;
interesting to see where the MCU takes him.

